{"parentUuid":"cdde2f61-1f7b-4cfe-b23b-78c6ef94a0d6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgwa4qQYfmipk8QYKi56d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The Caliper repo's `measurement.md` already covers the 2026-11-10 universe change, but the pages stay on November 9 until a weekly run in the new format happens. I'm reading the current text now so I only fix what is still stale.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1689,"cache_read_input_tokens":744543,"output_tokens":899,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":767},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1689,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":899,"cache_read_input_tokens":744543,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1689},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"28da613b-f3e3-48ff-9fbb-cb2f4076a869","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cgwa4pxWxjtRMhmheEWPK","requestRef":"beab4f10-012c-4e9b-b9b8-e56c8834775a","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"eadda6f7-bbc9-4f24-984a-159a96da316a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:55:42.585Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","session_id":"4c72440d-a6e2-4bf8-9c73-6f5da1acdec2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper","sessionId":"4c72440d-a6e2-4bf8-9c73-6f5da1acdec2","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"main","slug":"frolicking-percolating-waffle"}
{"parentUuid":"eadda6f7-bbc9-4f24-984a-159a96da316a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgwa4qQYfmipk8QYKi56d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XjMhASMwtDkUaVKDBUsWDu","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"/usr/bin/git show origin/main:docs/measurement.md | sed -n 96,140p","description":"Read the current headline and universe sections of measurement.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1689,"cache_read_input_tokens":744543,"output_tokens":899,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":767},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1689,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":899,"cache_read_input_tokens":744543,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1689},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XjMhASMwtDkUaVKDBUsWDu":{"command":"cd /Users/gabe/Projects/caliper && /usr/bin/git show origin/main:docs/measurement.md | sed -n 96,140p","description":"Read the current headline and universe sections of measurement.md"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01XjMhASMwtDkUaVKDBUsWDu":{"cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"28da613b-f3e3-48ff-9fbb-cb2f4076a869","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cgwa4pxWxjtRMhmheEWPK","requestRef":"beab4f10-012c-4e9b-b9b8-e56c8834775a","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"f0941ad0-8e83-4891-8f18-6aa78c4a42f1","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:55:42.587Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","session_id":"4c72440d-a6e2-4bf8-9c73-6f5da1acdec2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper","sessionId":"4c72440d-a6e2-4bf8-9c73-6f5da1acdec2","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"main","slug":"frolicking-percolating-waffle"}
{"parentUuid":"f0941ad0-8e83-4891-8f18-6aa78c4a42f1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"59b8b9da-3745-4467-ad83-b44577dbb5ac","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjMhASMwtDkUaVKDBUsWDu","type":"tool_result","content":"- **Agreement of the whole universe** (`agree_universe`): identical or spelled, over every prop in the universe. A prop the tool omits counts against it. This is the comparison between tools; Caliper's own headline is below.\n- **Agreement of listed props** (`agree_listed`) and the share the checker contradicts (`wrong_listed`): over the props the tool lists, which is what Caliper's own `agree` and `wrong` have always been.\n- **Omitted props**, by where the checker declares them (`missing_by_stratum`): the component's own file, another file of the corpus, or a dependency. A prop Caliper lists only as a member of a collapsed inheritance layer is counted apart (`collapsed`), because that is a choice and not an omission.\n- **Why a prop is omitted** (`missing_by_reason`): the tool reports no such component, it reports the component but not the prop, or it threw on the file.\n- **react-docgen's thrown files** (`failed_files`): files its default resolver throws on, 17% to 21% of the files on antd and shadcn. Their props are omitted, so they cost react-docgen agreement; they have their own column so the cost is visible.\n\n**What changed on 2026-11-10 (`oracle.universe_version` 2).** Until then the universe counted every such function, and a default export was keyed `default`. On the 2026-11-09 weekly, whole-universe agreement on react-spectrum read Caliper 30.7% against RDT 42.5% of 5,582 props, which read as Caliper being less complete. It was two artifacts, both measured on the saved dump of that run (`apps/oracle/tests/spectrum-universe.test.ts`):\n\n- RDT lists the props of functions that are no component, Caliper rightly does not: 874 props of 65 functions (42 lowercase helpers and 23 hooks), of which RDT agrees on 684 and Caliper lists none. 741 of them are declared in dependencies: 725 members of a lib type (`Element`, `String`) that the parameter takes, the rest a `@parcel/rsc` type. Without these 874, 4,708 props, Caliper agrees on 36.4% and RDT on 35.9%. The gap was RDT listing them.\n- The oracle keyed 535 components `default` (529 that Caliper reports under their own names, 6 it does not report), so neither tool's entry met theirs and both scored zero on their 2,613 props. Without those as well, 2,095 props, Caliper agrees on 81.8% and RDT on 80.6%. That figure leaves the `default` props out; what they read once named comes with the next weekly run, which was not repeated for this change.\n\nWith the shape rules themselves over the same dump (`default` kept, as it is named now), 4,718 props: Caliper 36.3%, RDT 36.0%. The rules take out 730 props of lib-typed parameters, 58 of hooks and 76 of lowercase helpers, and keep the 10 props of lowercase functions that carry their file's name. The rest of the gap to 81.8% is the default exports, which are in the universe now.\n\n`oracle.shared_props`, `agree` and `wrong` keep their meaning, over the props both tools list. Naming default exports lets Caliper's `Foo` meet the checker's `Foo`, so a corpus with default-exported components, spectrum above all, has more shared props than before (2,023 in the record of 2026-11-06); no other change reaches them. A record without `universe_version` is version 1: its `tools` count every function, with a default export named `default`. A record of version 2 keeps the scores over every function, default exports named, in `oracle.tools_all_functions`, and counts what was left out in `oracle.universe_excluded` (functions and props, by rule). The summary prints both, and the weekly does not read a rise in wrong types, or any other number of the oracle, against a record of another version.\n\nA local run on antd and shadcn at the commit that added the whole-universe comparison, over every function (version 1), with the default collapse (the weekly record collapses nothing, and reads 44.7% and 90.4% for Caliper), reads, for the whole universe: Caliper 39.7% and 40.1% (its `collapsed` layers and any component it does not report count against it), RDT 44.8% and 84.8%, react-docgen 24.6% and 1.9%. This comparison between tools is information, never a gate, and the rules were validated on antd and shadcn; the weekly run produces all five corpora.\n\n### The headline\n\nWhat matters is whether the types Storybook and agents are shown are right, not whether Caliper matches RDT. The headline (`scripts/headline.py`, read from `oracle.tools.caliper_reported`, which `apps/oracle/src/tool-score.ts` `scoreReported` computes) takes every own prop the checker finds on a component Caliper reports, so a function the checker takes for a component and Caliper rightly ignores does not count against it, and sorts each prop into one kind:\n\n- **Correct**: the same type as the checker, or the same type spelled differently.\n- **Less precise**: vaguer than the checker's (a name left unexpanded, `string` for a union of literals), not contradicting it.\n- **Wrong**: describes something else. This is the gate.\n- **No checker type**: the checker has none either (an unresolved import, `any`).\n- **Missing**: Caliper reports the component but not the prop.\n- **Named only**: Caliper lists the prop by name as a member of a collapsed inheritance layer, so it is surfaced but a reader sees no type for it. It is counted apart, as neither correct nor wrong, because correct values reached through a layer are a win and a bare name is not.\n\nThe record keeps the counts per corpus as `headline`, next to the unchanged `oracle` and `recall` fields. The shares in the summary are of every prop of the components Caliper reports. The gate uses the wrong share of the props whose type can be judged (correct, less precise and wrong), so a corpus with many layer-only props does not dilute it.\n\nThe issue opens when, on any corpus:\n\n- more than 0.1% of the judged props are wrong, or the wrong share is higher than last week's (the count is not trustworthy in either direction yet: the comparator reads an index signature as a property and so reports wrong props that are right, and it scores a dropped `Partial` or `Omit` as less precise or identical, so wrong answers never reach it; `docs/rdt-differences.md`);\n- a question a reader is asked is answered silent-wrong (`scripts/agent_eval.py`);\n- a whole run is over a budget: 2 s or 600 MiB with dependencies loaded, the default (`BUDGETS` in `scripts/headline.py`). The budgets are user-facing limits, not week-over-week ones; Chakra, the largest corpus, ran in 0.24 s and 378 MiB in the weekly run of 2026-11-06.\n\nRecall against RDT stays information. A local run on antd and shadcn at the commit that added this, with the default collapse, reads: antd 83.7% correct, 6.0% less precise, 0.00% wrong, 10.2% named only (3,426 props); shadcn 54.4% correct, 5.1% less precise, 0.06% wrong (0.096% of the judged props, just under the gate), 0.4% missing, 39.6% named only (13,918 props). The weekly record collapses nothing (`--collapse-inherited-props none`), so named only is 0 there; its run of 2026-11-06 reads antd 94.0% correct, 6.0% less precise, 0 wrong, and shadcn 93.8% correct, 5.3% less precise, 8 wrong (0.058% of the shared props), 0.4% missing (`headline` in `weekly.json`, `main` at `c5fbb33e`). Mantine, Chakra and Spectrum read in that run: 90.9%, 100.0% and 83.7% agree, with 0, 1 and 22 wrong. Of Spectrum's 22, the comparator's index-signature artifact accounts for all (`docs/rdt-differences.md`), so that gate firing is false until the comparator is corrected.\n\n### An independent reference\n\nThe oracle above scores a tool against the TypeScript checker alone. That cannot judge a mode that uses the checker, which would agree with itself, and it cannot say which side is right when they differ. `scripts/reference_sample.py` (`apps/oracle/src/reference.ts`) judges a sample of props by two methods that fail differently:\n\n- **A, the checker's type.** The same normalized type the oracle compares (`oracle-checker.ts`).\n- **B, the declared type.** What the prop's declaration writes, read from source text with TypeScript's parser (`reference-declared.ts`), never from the checker's type printer. The checker is asked only where the declaration is. B abstains where the declaration leaves the type to be computed (an indexed access, `keyof`, a conditional type), writes no type (an unannotated member, a method with no return type, or a value such as `count: 1` in an object literal that the checker infers the type of), has no declaration (a mapped type's synthesized member), or has several that differ.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5e5158e6-ae6a-41f2-9081-f2e61972d438","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:55:42.783Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"- **Agreement of the whole universe** (`agree_universe`): identical or spelled, over every prop in the universe. A prop the tool omits counts against it. This is the comparison between tools; Caliper's own headline is below.\n- **Agreement of listed props** (`agree_listed`) and the share the checker contradicts (`wrong_listed`): over the props the tool lists, which is what Caliper's own `agree` and `wrong` have always been.\n- **Omitted props**, by where the checker declares them (`missing_by_stratum`): the component's own file, another file of the corpus, or a dependency. A prop Caliper lists only as a member of a collapsed inheritance layer is counted apart (`collapsed`), because that is a choice and not an omission.\n- **Why a prop is omitted** (`missing_by_reason`): the tool reports no such component, it reports the component but not the prop, or it threw on the file.\n- **react-docgen's thrown files** (`failed_files`): files its default resolver throws on, 17% to 21% of the files on antd and shadcn. Their props are omitted, so they cost react-docgen agreement; they have their own column so the cost is visible.\n\n**What changed on 2026-11-10 (`oracle.universe_version` 2).** Until then the universe counted every such function, and a default export was keyed `default`. On the 2026-11-09 weekly, whole-universe agreement on react-spectrum read Caliper 30.7% against RDT 42.5% of 5,582 props, which read as Caliper being less complete. It was two artifacts, both measured on the saved dump of that run (`apps/oracle/tests/spectrum-universe.test.ts`):\n\n- RDT lists the props of functions that are no component, Caliper rightly does not: 874 props of 65 functions (42 lowercase helpers and 23 hooks), of which RDT agrees on 684 and Caliper lists none. 741 of them are declared in dependencies: 725 members of a lib type (`Element`, `String`) that the parameter takes, the rest a `@parcel/rsc` type. Without these 874, 4,708 props, Caliper agrees on 36.4% and RDT on 35.9%. The gap was RDT listing them.\n- The oracle keyed 535 components `default` (529 that Caliper reports under their own names, 6 it does not report), so neither tool's entry met theirs and both scored zero on their 2,613 props. Without those as well, 2,095 props, Caliper agrees on 81.8% and RDT on 80.6%. That figure leaves the `default` props out; what they read once named comes with the next weekly run, which was not repeated for this change.\n\nWith the shape rules themselves over the same dump (`default` kept, as it is named now), 4,718 props: Caliper 36.3%, RDT 36.0%. The rules take out 730 props of lib-typed parameters, 58 of hooks and 76 of lowercase helpers, and keep the 10 props of lowercase functions that carry their file's name. The rest of the gap to 81.8% is the default exports, which are in the universe now.\n\n`oracle.shared_props`, `agree` and `wrong` keep their meaning, over the props both tools list. Naming default exports lets Caliper's `Foo` meet the checker's `Foo`, so a corpus with default-exported components, spectrum above all, has more shared props than before (2,023 in the record of 2026-11-06); no other change reaches them. A record without `universe_version` is version 1: its `tools` count every function, with a default export named `default`. A record of version 2 keeps the scores over every function, default exports named, in `oracle.tools_all_functions`, and counts what was left out in `oracle.universe_excluded` (functions and props, by rule). The summary prints both, and the weekly does not read a rise in wrong types, or any other number of the oracle, against a record of another version.\n\nA local run on antd and shadcn at the commit that added the whole-universe comparison, over every function (version 1), with the default collapse (the weekly record collapses nothing, and reads 44.7% and 90.4% for Caliper), reads, for the whole universe: Caliper 39.7% and 40.1% (its `collapsed` layers and any component it does not report count against it), RDT 44.8% and 84.8%, react-docgen 24.6% and 1.9%. This comparison between tools is information, never a gate, and the rules were validated on antd and shadcn; the weekly run produces all five corpora.\n\n### The headline\n\nWhat matters is whether the types Storybook and agents are shown are right, not whether Caliper matches RDT. The headline (`scripts/headline.py`, read from `oracle.tools.caliper_reported`, which `apps/oracle/src/tool-score.ts` `scoreReported` computes) takes every own prop the checker finds on a component Caliper reports, so a function the checker takes for a component and Caliper rightly ignores does not count against it, and sorts each prop into one kind:\n\n- **Correct**: the same type as the checker, or the same type spelled differently.\n- **Less precise**: vaguer than the checker's (a name left unexpanded, `string` for a union of literals), not contradicting it.\n- **Wrong**: describes something else. This is the gate.\n- **No checker type**: the checker has none either (an unresolved import, `any`).\n- **Missing**: Caliper reports the component but not the prop.\n- **Named only**: Caliper lists the prop by name as a member of a collapsed inheritance layer, so it is surfaced but a reader sees no type for it. It is counted apart, as neither correct nor wrong, because correct values reached through a layer are a win and a bare name is not.\n\nThe record keeps the counts per corpus as `headline`, next to the unchanged `oracle` and `recall` fields. The shares in the summary are of every prop of the components Caliper reports. The gate uses the wrong share of the props whose type can be judged (correct, less precise and wrong), so a corpus with many layer-only props does not dilute it.\n\nThe issue opens when, on any corpus:\n\n- more than 0.1% of the judged props are wrong, or the wrong share is higher than last week's (the count is not trustworthy in either direction yet: the comparator reads an index signature as a property and so reports wrong props that are right, and it scores a dropped `Partial` or `Omit` as less precise or identical, so wrong answers never reach it; `docs/rdt-differences.md`);\n- a question a reader is asked is answered silent-wrong (`scripts/agent_eval.py`);\n- a whole run is over a budget: 2 s or 600 MiB with dependencies loaded, the default (`BUDGETS` in `scripts/headline.py`). The budgets are user-facing limits, not week-over-week ones; Chakra, the largest corpus, ran in 0.24 s and 378 MiB in the weekly run of 2026-11-06.\n\nRecall against RDT stays information. A local run on antd and shadcn at the commit that added this, with the default collapse, reads: antd 83.7% correct, 6.0% less precise, 0.00% wrong, 10.2% named only (3,426 props); shadcn 54.4% correct, 5.1% less precise, 0.06% wrong (0.096% of the judged props, just under the gate), 0.4% missing, 39.6% named only (13,918 props). The weekly record collapses nothing (`--collapse-inherited-props none`), so named only is 0 there; its run of 2026-11-06 reads antd 94.0% correct, 6.0% less precise, 0 wrong, and shadcn 93.8% correct, 5.3% less precise, 8 wrong (0.058% of the shared props), 0.4% missing (`headline` in `weekly.json`, `main` at `c5fbb33e`). Mantine, Chakra and Spectrum read in that run: 90.9%, 100.0% and 83.7% agree, with 0, 1 and 22 wrong. Of Spectrum's 22, the comparator's index-signature artifact accounts for all (`docs/rdt-differences.md`), so that gate firing is false until the comparator is corrected.\n\n### An independent reference\n\nThe oracle above scores a tool against the TypeScript checker alone. That cannot judge a mode that uses the checker, which would agree with itself, and it cannot say which side is right when they differ. `scripts/reference_sample.py` (`apps/oracle/src/reference.ts`) judges a sample of props by two methods that fail differently:\n\n- **A, the checker's type.** The same normalized type the oracle compares (`oracle-checker.ts`).\n- **B, the declared type.** What the prop's declaration writes, read from source text with TypeScript's parser (`reference-declared.ts`), never from the checker's type printer. The checker is asked only where the declaration is. B abstains where the declaration leaves the type to be computed (an indexed access, `keyof`, a conditional type), writes no type (an unannotated member, a method with no return type, or a value such as `count: 1` in an object literal that the checker infers the type of), has no declaration (a mapped type's synthesized member), or has several that differ.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"28da613b-f3e3-48ff-9fbb-cb2f4076a869","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper","root":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"orin-dx/caliper","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","r